# Local Worker secrets. Copy to `.dev.vars` (gitignored) and fill in.
#
# In a deployed environment these are set with `wrangler secret put <NAME>`.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET must never appear in wrangler.jsonc and must never be
# committed — it is the one credential that lets anyone mint sign-ins as us.

# The "Web application" OAuth client from Google Cloud Console → Credentials.
# Add an authorized redirect URI for every origin you sign in from, e.g.
#   http://localhost:5173/api/auth/google/callback
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# Optional. Pins the OAuth callback origin when the Worker sits behind a proxy
# that rewrites the Host header. Defaults to the request's own origin.
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN="https://nuggbudz.example.com"

# Liveness windows for the pool, in seconds. Production values live in
# wrangler.jsonc (15 min of quiet before a queue entry is dropped, 10 min before
# an unconfirmed match is cancelled). Shrink them here to watch the expiry path
# run in seconds — `pnpm smoke` runs its expiry checks only when the queue
# window is 20s or less.
# QUEUE_IDLE_SECONDS="6"
# QUEUE_WARN_LEAD_SECONDS="3"
# MATCH_CONFIRM_SECONDS="8"

# Socket upgrades one client may make per minute. The limiter keys on
# `CF-Connecting-IP`, which `pnpm dev` never sets, so locally EVERY client shares
# the `unknown` bucket — and `pnpm test:e2e` opens several dozen sockets a minute
# from one machine. Raise it here to run the suites; the production figure lives
# in wrangler.jsonc and is sized for a venue NAT, not for a test runner. Hitting
# it looks like "Lost the connection. Try again." rather than like a refusal.
# POOL_UPGRADE_LIMIT="300"
#
# The same, for upgrades with no session — a separate, tighter bucket since
# signed-out visitors can browse the market (#150). Every landing page opens one.
# POOL_ANON_UPGRADE_LIMIT="300"

# Stripe. Both of these are secrets in a deployed environment
# (`wrangler secret put`), and a pool with either of them missing REFUSES TO
# PAIR rather than pairing for free — see the README's "Payments" section.
# The publishable key is not here: it is a build-time env var
# (`VITE_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY`), public by design.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=""
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# Pair without charging anybody. Required for `pnpm dev`, `pnpm smoke` and
# `pnpm test:e2e` on a checkout with no Stripe keys — without it, joining the
# pool is refused with `payment_unavailable`.
#
# Checked in ADDITION to the two secrets above being empty, never instead of
# them, so an empty production secret is never indistinguishable from
# intentional test mode. Ignored entirely when Stripe is configured.
ALLOW_UNCHARGED_PAIRING="1"

# Who may list and resolve disputed pickups, and retry a refund Stripe refused:
# comma-separated `users.id` values, checked *in addition to* a real signed-in
# session (see the README's "Disputes" and "Holds" sections). Unset means there
# are no operators at all and every `/api/admin/*` route answers 404, which is
# the shape a checkout is in.
#
# Two lanes seed an operator fixture and each uses its OWN id, because they run
# against different servers with different session stores. Uncomment whichever
# you are running; both may be listed at once, comma-separated.
#
#   `pnpm smoke`        — dispute-resolution checks (SMOKE_REQUIRE_ADMIN)
# OPERATOR_USER_IDS="5eed5eed-0000-4000-8000-000000000033"
#   `pnpm payment-gate` — holds queue and refund retry (PAYMENT_GATE_REQUIRE_ADMIN)
# OPERATOR_USER_IDS="9a1d9a1d-0000-4000-8000-000000000033"

# Seat honeypot buyers: decoys that populate an empty market and act as an abuse
# tripwire. Off by default EVERYWHERE, including on a charged production
# deployment — this var answers exactly one question and nothing else implies it.
#
# A decoy appears in the waiting count and as a dot, can be matched, and can hold
# a short canned conversation; it can never be charged (the money gate answers
# `honeypot` before Stripe is consulted), never releases a pickup code, and
# therefore can never settle or be disputed. It excuses itself within 45s through
# the refunding teardown. See the README's "Honeypot buyers" section.
#
# Turn it on to see a populated market from one browser; leave it off to run any
# suite that expects an empty one.
# HONEYPOT_BUYERS="1"

# Where the Stripe REST calls go. Only ever set to point at
# `scripts/fake-stripe.mjs`, which is how the charged path is driven end to end
# without a Stripe account. Unset means the real API.
# STRIPE_API_BASE="http://127.0.0.1:5312/v1"
